TI  - BEYOND BLACK OCTOBER: AN AI-DRIVEN PREDICTIVE CYBER DEFENSE FRAMEWORK FOR PREVENTING AUTONOMOUS EXPLOITATION OF CRITICAL SOFTWARE VULNERABILITIES
AU  - Ashikur Rahman
T2  - International Journal of Engineering Technology Research & Management
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-11-01
DO  - 10.67809/ijetrm.bko2611r8x4
UR  - https://doi.org/10.67809/ijetrm.bko2611r8x4
AB  - The rapid advancement of artificial intelligence (AI) is transforming cybersecurity by enabling automated vulnerability discovery, exploit development, threat analysis, and defensive security operations. However, these capabilities also introduce significa nt risks when sophisticated AI systems are used to identify and exploit weaknesses in widely deployed software. The October 2026 PHP security crisis, commonly described in media coverage as Black October, illustrates this emerging security challenge. Two c ritical vulnerabilities, CVE - 2026 - 109618 and CVE - 2026 - 109619, affected the PHP runtime and created opportunities for unauthenticated remote code execution. The vulnerabilities were addressed through security releases on October 21, 2026, following AI - assis ted discovery involving Wiz Research and Google's Gemini technology. This research examines the relationship between advanced AI - assisted vulnerability discovery and the growing speed of automated cyber exploitation. It investigates the technical characteristics of the PHP vulnerabilities, the limitations of conventional vu lnerability management, the operational challenges facing small and medium - sized organizations, and the implications of autonomous AI agents for global cybersecurity. The paper proposes an AI - Driven Predictive Cyber Defense Framework (AIPCDF), a multilayer security architecture integrating AI - assisted vulnerability assessment, software dependency intelligence, continuous attack - surface monitoring, behavioral anomaly det ection, automated patch prioritization, zero - trust access controls, and human - supervised incident response. The proposed framework emphasizes preventive security rather than relying exclusively on emergency patching after public disclosure. A conceptual research methodology is developed to evaluate the proposed framework using vulnerability detection accuracy, mean time to remediation, false - positive rates, exploit detection latency, and recovery performance. The paper does not claim experime ntal validation; instead, it establishes a reproducible evaluation design for future implementation. The findings from the case analysis suggest that the next generation of cybersecurity must combine secure software engineering, responsible AI deployment, automated threat intelligence, and organizational resilience. The proposed framework offers a practic al direction for protecting critical digital infrastructure against increasingly capable AI - assisted cyber threats.
