TI  - Self-Operating Code Refactoring and Vulnerability Detection Using Static Analysis and Machine Learning Models
AU  - Sachin Sharma
T2  - International Journal of Research Publication and Reviews
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-10-31
DO  - 10.55248/gengpi.07.1026.35028
UR  - https://doi.org/10.55248/gengpi.07.1026.35028
AB  - Modern enterprise software development faces unprecedented challenges due to the rapid accumulation of technical debt and security vulnerabilities. Traditional static application security testing (SAST) tools suffer from high false-positive rates and lack automated remediation capabilities. Conversely, pure machine learning (ML) models often generate syntactically incorrect code or fail to ensure program semantic equivalence during refactoring. This paper proposes AutoRefact-Sec, an autonomous, self-operating hybrid framework that unifies deterministic static analysis with probabilistic Transformer-based Deep Learning models. By combining Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) representation, Control Flow Graphs (CFG), and Data Flow Graphs (DFG) with a fine-tuned Graph Neural Network (GNN) and CodeBERT backend, AutoRefact-Sec achieves real-time vulnerability detection (CWE identification) and performs context-aware, safety-guaranteed code refactoring without human intervention. We evaluate our framework on a dataset of 150,000 open-source C/C++ and Java functions. Experimental results indicate a 94.2% precision rate in vulnerability detection and an 88.6% success rate in autonomous refactoring validation via symbolic execution and automated regression testing.
