TY  - GEN
TI  - Artificial Intelligence for Security Analysis of Smart Contracts: A Systematic Review
AU  - Lívia Carrera
AU  - Raquel Coelho Gomes Pinto
AU  - Anderson Fernandes Pereira dos Santos
T2  - Brazilian Symposium on Multimedia and the Web
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026-10-27
DO  - 10.5753/webmedia.2026.30821
UR  - https://doi.org/10.5753/webmedia.2026.30821
AB  - This Systematic Literature Review (SLR) investigates the application of Artificial Intelligence (AI) techniques for smart contract vulnerability detection in Web3 decentralized applications (dApps). Smart contracts enable trustless finance, decentralized governance, and autonomous economic protocols; however, their immutability makes vulnerabilities deployed on-chain difficult to patch without costly governance interventions. Between 2020 and 2024, 127 high-impact attacks caused $2.3 billion in cumulative losses, while state-of-the-art automated tools could have prevented only 8% of them (10 of 127, $149M). Following PRISMA guidelines, records retrieved from IEEE Xplore, the ACM Digital Library, and Springer Link were screened, resulting in 93 primary studies selected for in-depth analysis. The review organizes the literature around three generations of AI-based detection: classical machine learning, deep learning and Graph Neural Networks (GNNs), and Large Language Models (LLMs). The findings show that LLM-based detectors often achieve high recall but limited precision, while GNNs obtain strong results on narrowly scoped economic attacks, such as sandwiching, but still struggle to generalize. Logic-layer and protocol-layer vulnerabilities, which account for a significant share of financial losses, remain insufficiently addressed. The review also highlights emerging directions, including cross-contract reasoning, automated repair, and the security implications of the post-quantum cryptography transition. Persistent challenges include dataset scarcity, limited explainability, weak generalization, and the lack of robust methods for complex cross-contract and protocol-level vulnerabilities. These insights provide guidance for future research toward more accurate, explainable, and comprehensive AI-based security analysis of smart contracts.
