DA  - 2026-10-28
DO  - 10.1016/j.nexus.2026.100891
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nexus.2026.100891
AB  - The global shift toward sustainable energy sources has highlighted the prospects of solar energy in agricultural applications, particularly in farm-produce drying. With the world population projected to reach 9.1 billion by 2050, food production must increase by approximately 70%, even as food loss and waste remain around 30 %. These projections have intensified the search for energy-efficient and sustainable food preservation technologies. Automated solar hybrid drying represents an environmentally responsible solution aligned with the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs 1, 2, 7, 11 and 12). The review discusses hybrid solar dryers integrated with automatic control systems such as Arduino, Raspberry Pi, and IoT-based sensor networks for monitoring and regulating temperature, humidity, and airflow. Reported systems commonly operate within drying-air temperature ranges of approximately 40–70°C, with humidity and temperature-dependent airflow regulation used to improve drying uniformity, reduce overheating, and enhance product quality. The review further evaluates auxiliary heat integration, including solar–electric, solar–biomass, solar–gas, and thermal-energy-storage-assisted configurations, emphasizing their effects on drying continuity, energy use, cost, and sustainability. Comparative analysis identifies the strengths and limitations of automated solar hybrid dryers in terms of control architecture, efficiency, scalability, and economic feasibility. Future directions include artificial intelligence, emerging sensor technologies, and energy-storage integration for fully autonomous solar hybrid dryers. Overall, this review contributes to advancing sustainable agricultural processing technologies that reduce food loss and food waste, promote clean energy, and support SDGs global food security targets toward 2050.
